# Optional LLM relevance-triage for the literature radar (scripts/llm_triage.py).
# Same variable names as the dreamcatcher ai-worker — copy your .env values here,
# then set them as GitHub Actions repo secrets (Settings → Secrets and variables →
# Actions). If none are set, the radar stays keyless and keeps every candidate.
#
# Fallback order: NVIDIA NIM (tier 1) → Groq (tier 2) → Cloudflare Workers AI (tier 3).
# Model ids are NOT pinned: each tier queries the provider's live model list and
# the report names the model that actually answered. Set a *_MODEL var only to
# reproduce an earlier run. In CI these are repo *variables*, not secrets.
# Any single one is enough; set as many as you like for resilience.

# Tier 1 — NVIDIA NIM (integrate.api.nvidia.com), OpenAI-compatible
NVIDIA_API_KEY=
# NIM_MODEL=                                  # optional PIN; unset = discover a live id

# Tier 2 — Groq (api.groq.com/openai/v1), OpenAI-compatible
GROQ_API_KEY=
# GROQ_MODEL=                                 # optional PIN; unset = discover a live id
#   (the old default `llama-3.1-8b-instant` 404s since Groq retired every Llama in 2026-08 —
#    that is exactly why nothing is pinned by default any more)

# Tier 3 — Cloudflare Workers AI via the REST API (needs BOTH). The dreamcatcher
# worker uses the in-Worker `AI` binding; from a GitHub Action we call the REST API,
# which needs an account id + an API token scoped to "Workers AI: Read".
CF_ACCOUNT_ID=
CF_API_TOKEN=
# CF_AI_MODEL=                                # optional PIN; unset = discover a live id

# Literature radar — optional contact address for the Europe PMC User-Agent.
# Unset → the UA carries the repo URL. In CI this is a repo *variable*.
# LITWATCH_CONTACT=
